(defxdoc deftreeset-implementation
  :parents (deftreeset)
  :short "Implementation of @(tsee deftreeset)."
  :long
  (xdoc::topstring
   (xdoc::p
    "This book is included by @('centaur/fty/deftypes')
     like the other type generators,
     but unlike @('fty-set'), whose oset library comes bundled,
     it does not include the treeset library:
     the generator functions only build event forms,
     so the treeset functions and rules they mention are just quoted symbols
     (the treeset package comes from this book's portcullis).
     The library is instead supplied
     by the opt-in @('kestrel/fty/deftreeset') book,
     whose absence @('parse-flextreeset') detects with a friendly error.")
   (xdoc::p
    "Unlike the other generators,
     a treeset member contributes <emph>two</emph>
     mutually recursive defines to a clique
     (deftypes appends per-member define lists): @('tree-all-<name>'),
     a structural fold over the raw binary tree, and the public recognizer,
     which conjoins @('treeset::setp') with the fold's first level
     inlined at @('(treeset::fix x)').
     The inlining lets the clique admit with plain @('acl2-count') measures,
     since the fold is only ever applied to proper subtrees.
     Since a mutual recursion takes only one goal hint
     for its joint termination and guard proofs,
     the fold define carries the hints for the whole clique.")
   (xdoc::p
    "The theorems about the recognizer
     and count over the treeset operations
     are obtained by functional instantiation
     of the library's generic theories
     (@('generic-typed') and @('generic-count') in @('kestrel/data/treeset')),
     through a local min/delete view of the recognizer
     and a local structural fold for the count.
     The min/delete view of the recognizer
     is also exported as a disabled @(':definition') rule.")))

